const faq = [
  { q: 'Are there many ravens in Iceland?', a: 'Yes — Iceland has an exceptionally high density of Common Ravens relative to its land area, making it one of the most raven-saturated places on Earth. The harsh subarctic climate, abundant coastal and inland carrion (marine animals, sheep, fish waste), and relatively low persecution combine to maintain a thriving raven population. Ravens are visible almost everywhere in Iceland — from city suburbs to remote highland fells.' },
  { q: 'What is the Icelandic word for raven?', a: 'The Icelandic word for raven is hrafn (pronounced approximately "HRAP-n"). This is the direct descendant of Old Norse hrafn, unchanged over more than a millennium. The word appears widely in Icelandic place names, personal names (Hrafnkell, "Raven-kettle"), and in the name of the settlement narrative Raven-Flóki (Hrafna-Flóki Vilgerðarson), the Norse explorer who used ravens to navigate to Iceland.' },
